Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=t^{2}.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=0$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=2 t.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(- \left(t^{2}\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=- t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(- \left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(2 t\right) \\ &=- 2 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{0}^{2}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{0}^{2}\left(- 3 t^{2}\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[- t^{3}\right]_{0}^{2} \\ &=\left(-8\right)-\left(0\right) \\ &=-8
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=t^{2} + 1.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=2$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=2 t.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(5 \left(t\right)^{2} \left(t^{2} + 1\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=5 t^{4} + 5 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(- \left(t\right)^{2} \left(\left(t^{2} + 1\right) - 1\right)\right)\cdot \left(2 t\right) \\ &=- 2 t^{5}
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{2}^{1}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{2}^{1}\left(- 2 t^{5} + 5 t^{4} + 5 t^{2}\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[- \frac{t^{6}}{3} + t^{5} + \frac{5 t^{3}}{3}\right]_{2}^{1} \\ &=\left(\frac{7}{3}\right)-\left(24\right) \\ &=- \frac{65}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth. Rearranging gives $x=y^3+1$.

$$
x(t)=t^{3} + 1,\qquad y(t)=t.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=0$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=3 t^{2},\qquad y'(t)=1.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(- \left(t^{3} + 1\right) \left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(3 t^{2}\right) \\ &=- 3 t^{6} - 3 t^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(\left(t^{3} + 1\right) + \left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=t^{3} + t + 1
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{1}^{0}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{1}^{0}\left(- 3 t^{6} - 2 t^{3} + t + 1\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[- \frac{3 t^{7}}{7} - \frac{t^{4}}{2} + \frac{t^{2}}{2} + t\right]_{1}^{0} \\ &=\left(0\right)-\left(\frac{4}{7}\right) \\ &=- \frac{4}{7}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=t^{3}.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=2$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=3 t^{2}.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(- \frac{1}{\left(t^{3}\right)}\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=- \frac{1}{t^{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(\frac{1}{\left(t\right)^{2}}\right)\cdot \left(3 t^{2}\right) \\ &=3
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{2}^{1}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{2}^{1}\left(3 - \frac{1}{t^{3}}\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[3 t + \frac{1}{2 t^{2}}\right]_{2}^{1} \\ &=\left(\frac{7}{2}\right)-\left(\frac{49}{8}\right) \\ &=- \frac{21}{8}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=t^{2}.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=2 t.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(2 \left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=2 t
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(\left(t^{2}\right)\right)\cdot \left(2 t\right) \\ &=2 t^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{1}^{2}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{1}^{2}\left(2 t^{3} + 2 t\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[\frac{t^{4}}{2} + t^{2}\right]_{1}^{2} \\ &=\left(12\right)-\left(\frac{3}{2}\right) \\ &=\frac{21}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=t^{2} + t.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=2$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=2 t + 1.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(- \left(t\right) \left(t^{2} + t\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=- t^{3} - t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(\left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(2 t + 1\right) \\ &=2 t^{2} + t
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{2}^{1}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{2}^{1}\left(- t^{3} + t^{2} + t\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[- \frac{t^{4}}{4} + \frac{t^{3}}{3} + \frac{t^{2}}{2}\right]_{2}^{1} \\ &=\left(\frac{7}{12}\right)-\left(\frac{2}{3}\right) \\ &=- \frac{1}{12}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=\left(t + 1\right)^{2}.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=2 \left(t + 1\right).
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(- \frac{1}{\left(\left(t + 1\right)^{2}\right)}\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=- \frac{1}{\left(t + 1\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(\frac{1}{\left(\left(t + 1\right)^{2}\right)}\right)\cdot \left(2 \left(t + 1\right)\right) \\ &=\frac{2}{t + 1}
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{1}^{2}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{1}^{2}\left(\frac{2}{t + 1} - \frac{1}{\left(t + 1\right)^{2}}\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[2 \ln{\left(t + 1 \right)} + \frac{1}{t + 1}\right]_{1}^{2} \\ &=\left(\frac{1}{3} + 2 \ln{\left(3 \right)}\right)-\left(\frac{1}{2} + 2 \ln{\left(2 \right)}\right) \\ &=- 2 \ln{\left(2 \right)} - \frac{1}{6} + 2 \ln{\left(3 \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t^{2},\qquad y(t)=2 t.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=2 t,\qquad y'(t)=2.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(\left(t^{2}\right) - \left(2 t\right)\right)\cdot \left(2 t\right) \\ &=2 t^{3} - 4 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(2 \left(t^{2}\right) + 2 \left(2 t\right)\right)\cdot \left(2\right) \\ &=4 t^{2} + 8 t
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{1}^{2}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{1}^{2}\left(2 t^{3} + 8 t\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[\frac{t^{4}}{2} + 4 t^{2}\right]_{1}^{2} \\ &=\left(24\right)-\left(\frac{9}{2}\right) \\ &=\frac{39}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t^{2},\qquad y(t)=t.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=2$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=2 t,\qquad y'(t)=1.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(\left(t^{2}\right)^{2} - \left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(2 t\right) \\ &=2 t^{5} - 2 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(3 \left(t^{2}\right) - 2 \left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=3 t^{2} - 2 t
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{2}^{1}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{2}^{1}\left(2 t^{5} + t^{2} - 2 t\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[\frac{t^{6}}{3} + \frac{t^{3}}{3} - t^{2}\right]_{2}^{1} \\ &=\left(- \frac{1}{3}\right)-\left(20\right) \\ &=- \frac{61}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=t^{2} + 1.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=2$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=2 t.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(- \left(t^{2} + 1\right)^{2}\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=- t^{4} - 2 t^{2} - 1
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(\left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(2 t\right) \\ &=2 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{2}^{1}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{2}^{1}\left(- t^{4} - 1\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[- \frac{t^{5}}{5} - t\right]_{2}^{1} \\ &=\left(- \frac{6}{5}\right)-\left(- \frac{42}{5}\right) \\ &=\frac{36}{5}
\end{aligned}
$$
